The following is a revised version of Bruce Holloway's battleship
game.  It is based on his latest (I think) version dated 3/21/86
that incorporated all known bugs fixes.  This was the version Bruce
said he thought was now "complete" (Ha!)

This revision makes two major changes:

	1) The game can now be played several different ways using
	   command line options, and
	2) a new way to play using "salvos" has been added.

The command line now looks like this:

	battle [ -b | -s ] [ -a ] [ -m ]

The first argument selects the game type:

	-b is Bruce's "blitz" game (known as cheating)
	-s is my "salvo" game (known as challenging)
	no first option means a standard game

The next two arguments simply turn on two operational modes:

	-a has the computer ask you whether a shot hits, this is
		off by default (opposite of Bruce's version)
	-m has the computer mark its "misses" on your board, and
		is off by default (just like Bruce's version)

    Example: battle -b -m	plays a blitz game and marks misses
	     battle -a -m	plays a standard game, asks you for
					hit/miss results and marks misses
	     battle -s		plays a salvo game
	     battle ?		prints a cryptic usage message


The new game type "salvo", is a popular variation of "battleship" that
allows a player one shot for each of his/her ships still afloat.  This
puts a premium scoring hits early and knocking out some ships and also
makes much harder the situation where you face a superior force with only
your "PT-boat".  Makes for quicker play, too.

Bugs cheerfully heard about, and maybe even fixed!

